From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wsclean-mx <wsclean-mx@localhost>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 08:11:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10/18] Metal probe: measure on real uv coverage instead of a
 synthetic spread

The synthetic uniform uv spread is the easy case for a gridding kernel: its
footprints rarely collide, so tile atomics go uncontended. Real LOFAR coverage
clusters - LOFAR_3C196.ms has 621 250 rows over ~20 000 distinct footprint
origins at the 4-arcsec configuration - and it is the clustered case that the
kernel has to survive.

* metal/uvw_file.h reads a casacore taql dump of UVW/DATA/FLAG (shared with the
  ducc0 CPU baseline, so both are timed on the same rows), drops flagged and
  non-finite rows, and reports the counts.
* mx-metal-probe --op realdata takes --uvw-file/--freq/--lambda-per-cell, scales
  metres to grid cells exactly as the CPU tool does, and reuses the round-trip
  measurement plus both differential checks.

Measured (440 745 usable rows): at 50.4 lambda/cell the grid disagrees with its
float64 twin by 2.11e-05, above the strict 1e-5 bound, because tens of rows per
cell with a wide flux dynamic range push float32 summation noise up - the same
effect the quiet-Sun science gate recorded. Degridding (4e-07) and the adjoint
identity (4.5e-06) still pass, so the pair stays adjoint.

